Java provides Thread.setPriority(int) to set a thread's priority. We will discuss here how this maps to Linux/Solaris/Windows and several blindcorners one needs to be 
aware of.


-> Linux  SCHED_OTHER is the class used by normal users. The priority is always zero and the kernel changes it at runtime based on your behaviour and nice params.
   So really the user can only provide nice values (using renice or schedtool) to say thread X or mine is way lower in priority than thread  Y. 
   In case you re wondering, threads are really processes in Linux so nice (which is a process param) applies well here.

   There are other classes too SCHED_BATCH/SCHED_IDLE refer my linux documentation on the same topic for details.
   SCHED_RR/SCHED_FIFO only root can assign these.

   Based on this bug report http://bugs.sun.com/bugdatabase/view_bug.do?bug_id=4813310 and this entry "Updated: January 30, 2007"...
      ... it appears that setpriority does do a renice of the kernel thread which IMO is really fine for SCHED_OTHER
<update   looking at os_linux.cpp set_native_priority they are invoking setpriority from resource.h which is a POSIX
          call to change a process' nice value... perfect this is exactly what we want
/update>


-> Solaris  Lots of scheduling classes but IA/TS are the ones exposed by hotspot. So the priority values you enter here do indeed get mapped to 
   the underlying thread priorities with some scaling of course. I believe jdk7 turns this off to avoid some starvation bug in Solaris where a 
   lower priority thread doesnt get enough boost to catch up with higher priority threads ping ponging between each other.
   http://blogs.sun.com/dave/entry/java_thread_priority_revisted_in

   -XX:+UseThreadPriorities to force enable

